annel","w":24,"h":24.4,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"button","id":"","cls":"channel-rail-item font-display text-subheading italic","name":"c channel","w":25.3,"h":24.4,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"button","id":"","cls":"channel-rail-item font-display text-subheading italic","name":"h channel","w":24.1,"h":24.4,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"button","id":"","cls":"channel-rail-item font-display text-subheading italic","name":"alpha channel","w":24.6,"h":25.4,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"span","id":"","cls":"slider-thumb glass-specular-track","name":"L channel","w":563.8,"h":24,"attributedTo":"span.glass-slider.channel-slider"},{"route":"/","tag":"span","id":"","cls":"slider-thumb glass-specular-track","name":"C channel","w":563.8,"h":24,"attributedTo":"span.glass-slider.channel-slider"},{"route":"/","tag":"span","id":"","cls":"slider-thumb glass-specular-track","name":"H channel","w":563.8,"h":24,"attributedTo":"span.glass-slider.channel-slider"},{"route":"/","tag":"span","id":"","cls":"slider-thumb glass-specular-track","name":"ALPHA channel","w":563.8,"h":24,"attributedTo":"span.glass-slider.channel-slider"},{"route":"/","tag":"button","id":"","cls":"control-surface glass-control-edge glass-capsule-hover tap-squish focus-ring cursor-pointer items-center justify-between rounded-pill px-3 py-2 text-dropdown placeholder:text-muted-foreground disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-disabled [&>span]:line-clamp-1 transition-control aria-invalid:border-(--destructive) user-invalid:border-(--destructive) focus-visible:aria-invalid:shadow-(--invalid-ring) focus-visible:user-invalid:shadow-(--invalid-ring) space-trigger inline-flex w-fit h-fit align-baseline font-display italic tracking-tight select-none [&>span]:overflow-visible [&>span]:line-clamp-none [&>span]:block [&_svg]:translate-y-[0.06em] space-trigger--inline","name":"Select color space","w":144.4,"h":46.9,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"div","id":"","cls":"fading-scroll fading-scroll--y pane-scroll-fade flex flex-col flex-1 min-h-0 min-w-0 overflow-x-hidden","name":"DefinitionA cylindrical representation of the OKLab color space. Basic information Device dependency:Device-independentWhite point:D65Gamut:UnlimitedCreated:2019 Components L (Lightness)0% to 100%C (Chroma)0 to 0.5h (hue)0deg to 360deg Key properties Perceptual uniformity:Yes (inherited from OKLab)Hue linearity:YesLightness separation:Yes Conversion graph OKLChOKLabOKLChOKLabXYZOKLChOKLabXYZLabUsageCommon applications: Color selection, Color harmony, Gradient creationIndustries: Web design, User interface design, Digital artDetailed GuideHistorical ContextOKLCh is the polar form of OKLab, published by Björn Ottosson in 2020 to address the perceptual non-uniformities in older Lab/LCh models. The relationship is the same—Cartesian a/b re-expressed as chroma and hue—but the underlying OKLab space produces better uniformity, especially in blues and purples where CIE L*a*b* shows significant error. OKLCh is a first-class color function in CSS Color Level 4.Key CharacteristicsBetter uniformity than CIE LCh. Equal chroma steps look more consistent across the hue circle.Polar coordinates. Lightness, chroma, and hue—same intuitive axes as LCh.Stable lightness. Changing chroma or hue doesn’t shift perceived brightness the way it does in HSL.CSS-native. oklch() is supported in all modern browsers as of 2024.AdvantagesMarkedly better perceptual uniformity than HSL, HSV, or CIE LChPerceived brightness stays stable when you adjust chroma or hueDirect CSS support—no conversion step for web useClean gradients and palette generation with minimal hue shiftDisadvantagesNewer; less support in legacy tools and older design softwareConversion chain is longer than RGB or HSL (OKLCh → OKLab → XYZ → linear RGB → sRGB)Not directly displayable—still needs gamut mapping to a device spaceThe 0–1 lightness scale differs from Lab/LCh’s 0–100, which can cause confusionColor ModelComponentsL (Lightness): 0 (black) to 1 (white). Note the 0–1 range, not 0–100 as in CIE L*.C (Chroma): 0 (achromatic) to roughly 0.4 for the most saturated colors. The scale is much smaller than CIE LCh’s.H (Hue): 0 to 360 degrees (or 0–1 normalized). Same angular concept as LCh, but hue spacing is more perceptually even—the “blue problem” in CIE LCh (where blues compress into a narrow hue range) is largely resolved.Relationship to OKLabOKLCh relates to OKLab exactly as CIE LCh relates to CIE Lab: same data, polar vs. Cartesian. C = sqrt(a^2 + b^2), H = atan2(b, a). The lightness channel is shared directly.Color ConversionsOKLCh to OKLabPolar to Cartesian—recover the a/b axes from chroma and hue:OKLab to OKLChCartesian to polar—extract chroma (magnitude) and hue (angle) from the a/b plane:OKLab to XYZInvert the two linear transforms around a cube operation. The cube reverses the perceptual cube-root compression applied in the forward path:XYZ to OKLabTwo matrix multiplications sandwiching a cube root. The first maps XYZ to LMS cone responses; the cube root models the perceptual non-linearity; the second projects into OKLab:ApplicationsCSS oklch(). Supported in Chrome, Safari, and Firefox; increasingly adopted for design systems.Design system palettes. Step through L or C to build scales that look evenly spaced to the human eye.Accessible color. Adjust L to meet WCAG contrast targets without disturbing hue or saturation.Perceptual gradients. Interpolating in OKLCh avoids the hue shifts and muddy midpoints of RGB or HSL blends.Data visualization. Build color ramps where perceptual distance tracks data distance.","w":461.5,"h":495.1,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"a","id":"","cls":"","name":"OKLab","w":52.6,"h":29.5,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"div","id":"","cls":"fading-scroll fading-scroll--x katex-row","name":"Formula","w":410.6,"h":224,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"div","id":"","cls":"fading-scroll fading-scroll--x katex-row","name":"Formula","w":410.6,"h":224,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"div","id":"","cls":"fading-scroll fading-scroll--x katex-row","name":"Formula","w":410.6,"h":224,"attributedTo":"self"},{"route":"/","tag":"div","id":"","cls":"fading-scroll fading-scroll--x katex-row","name":"Formula","w":410.6,"h":224,"attributedTo":"self"}]}

